architecture-decision-record:
  identifier: "shelishi_01"
  title: The schema layer supplies types at runtime, reusing the shapes sheni already defines
  status: proposed
  date: "2026-08-29"
  deciders:
    - felix
  superseded-by: ""
  context: |
    Sheni, the second layer, holds four groups of types and all four are
    closed. Primitives and simple types are enumerated in the crate. The
    complex group is a curated library of composite shapes -- an address, a
    month, an amount of money -- deliberately closed so that two applications
    using Sheni mean the same thing by an address without agreeing on one
    first. Collections are closed in their constructors.

    A curated library, by construction, holds only what is common to
    applications that have nothing else in common. That is criterion two in
    ADR sheni_03, and it is doing its job. But everything an application
    actually is -- its orders, its invoices, its device readings -- fails that
    criterion by definition and always will.

    So there has to be a way to declare types, and ADR sheni_05 settled where
    it does not go. It is not a fifth group of the type layer, because a
    user-declared struct is a struct and provenance is not a kind, and because
    a resolver compiled into the type layer is paid for by every consumer of
    `u8`. It is the third layer, and this crate.

    What is left to settle is what this layer borrows rather than what it
    invents.
  decision: |
    A schema-based type is an enum or a struct that an application declares,
    read at runtime from a SYON schema.

    It is the same enum and the same struct ADR sheni_03 defines. The same
    variants over a backing primitive, the same named and typed fields, the
    same rules about required fields, unrecognised keys, and declaration
    order. What differs is only where the definition comes from. This layer
    supplies definitions; it does not define a second kind of enum or a second
    kind of struct, and if the two ever disagree about what a struct is, sheni
    is right and this crate is broken.

    A schema is data. Definitions are written in SYON and read through
    `syon-parser` exactly as documents are, so a schema arrives at runtime
    from a file rather than being compiled in. The shape is a mapping of type
    name to definition:

        types:
          Colour:
            kind: enum
            backing: u8
            variants:
              red: 1
              green: 2
              blue: 3
          Order:
            kind: struct
            fields:
              placed_at:
                type: timestamp
              ship_to:
                type: address
              lines:
                type: list<Order_line>
              note:
                type: string
                optional: yes

    Note what `ship_to` and `lines` do. A declared type may name a built-in
    from any of sheni's four groups, so a schema starts from the curated
    library rather than from nothing, and an application's own types compose
    with the shared ones. That is the payoff for sheni having built group 3
    first, and it is why this layer depends on that one.

    This crate's type is sheni's plus declared names -- a built-in `Type` or a
    reference to a definition in the schema -- and it instantiates sheni's
    generic `CollectionType` at that type, per ADR sheni_05. A `list<Order>`
    is then sheni's list constructor holding this layer's element type, with
    no new constructor and nothing for sheni to know about.

    The same holds for the definitions themselves. ADR sheni_03 makes
    `StructShape<T>` and `EnumShape` generic for this reason, so a declared
    struct is sheni's struct shape instantiated at this layer's type rather
    than a second implementation of the same idea. The sentence above -- that
    if the two disagree sheni is right and this crate is broken -- is then
    enforced by there being one struct, not by review. Reading a declared
    struct is sheni's struct read, and the type-expression grammar that parses
    `list<Order_line>` is sheni's grammar with this layer's name resolution
    supplied, per ADR sheni_04.

    Backing values on a declared enum are always explicit, as they are in the
    curated library. There is no implicit numbering from zero, for the reason
    ADR sheni_01 refused a default integer width: an implicit value is still a
    value, just one nobody wrote down, and with auto-numbering a reordering of
    the variant list silently changes what every stored value means.

    A declared name may not shadow a built-in. A schema cannot define `date`,
    `u8`, or `address`, so a type name means one thing and resolution never
    depends on which lookup ran first. The reverse also holds and is the
    sharper constraint: adding a type to sheni's curated library can break a
    schema that already uses that name, so every addition under ADR sheni_03
    is a compatibility event for this crate, not only a new shape.

    Resolution is a pass over the whole schema, run once when it is loaded,
    not lazily when a document happens to reach a field. A schema that names a
    type nothing defines is broken whether or not any document exercises it,
    and reporting that as a document error blames the wrong input.

    Definitions may refer to each other, including cyclically. A cycle every
    path of which runs through required struct fields describes a value that
    cannot be finite, and is rejected during resolution. A collection breaks a
    cycle, because an empty list terminates -- which is why ADR sheni_04 was
    careful to say an empty collection is a valid value.

    An optional field does not break a cycle, which an earlier draft of this
    record had wrong. ADR sheni_03 settled that optional means falling back to
    the type's own value, and that a struct has a fallback only when every one
    of its fields does. A cyclic definition therefore has no fallback to
    compute -- working one out requires the very type being defined -- so it
    cannot appear as an optional field in the first place, and the cycle is
    still there for the required-field check to reject. A collection remains
    the only thing that breaks one.

    Errors take the 501-599 band that ADR sheni_05 reserved, and they divide
    in two. A schema that does not resolve is a defect in the schema, reported
    with no document in hand and no path. A document that does not match a
    resolved schema is reported exactly as sheni reports one, with the same
    codes and the same path. Keeping those apart matters because they have
    different audiences.
  consequences:
    positive:
      - An application declares the types the curated library will never hold, without waiting for an ADR or a release.
      - A schema being data means validation can be shipped, versioned, and changed independently of the binary that enforces it, which is the capability that made the idea worth keeping.
      - Declared types compose with built-in ones, so a schema starts from a vocabulary rather than from primitives, and two applications still agree on the parts that came from sheni.
      - Reusing sheni's enum and struct wholesale means that machinery is exercised against a fixed, in-tree set before it ever meets a definition from outside.
      - Resolving eagerly turns a broken schema into an error about the schema, at load, rather than an error about whichever document first reached the broken field.
    negative:
      - This crate carries the name environment, the resolution pass, and the cycle detector, which is far more machinery than a lookup, and a class of error that has nothing to do with any document.
      - Cycle detection is a graph problem, and an over-strict rule rejects legitimate recursive schemas such as a tree of nodes.
      - Growing sheni's curated library can break a schema that had claimed the same name, so the two layers are coupled in a direction that is easy to forget.
      - A schema supplied at runtime is input, and everything that is true of parsing untrusted input becomes true of loading one.
  alternatives-rejected:
    -
      name: Define types in Rust, with a derive macro over existing structs
      reason: Idiomatic and less code, but a schema compiled into the binary cannot be supplied by a user, versioned separately, or read alongside the file being validated. That is the entire capability this layer exists to provide.
    -
      name: Reuse serde's Deserialize as the struct mechanism
      reason: Rejected for the reason ADR sheni_01 rejected `serde_json::Value` as the value model. Serde is a codegen path from a known Rust type, and here there is no known Rust type.
    -
      name: Give this layer its own enum and struct semantics, tuned for declared types
      reason: Two answers to what a struct is, differing in ways nobody would document. The point of this layer is a different source for the same shapes, and a divergence would make the library and the schema untranslatable to each other.
    -
      name: Let a declared type shadow a built-in of the same name
      reason: It would make resolution order observable, and it would let a schema quietly redefine `date` for every document read against it. Reserving the built-in names costs a rename and buys one meaning per name.
    -
      name: Resolve type names lazily, the first time a document reaches one
      reason: It turns a defect in the schema into an error about a document, blames the wrong input, and surfaces only if some document happens to exercise that field.
    -
      name: Detect cycles lazily, when a document happens to recurse
      reason: The same fault as lazy resolution, with the added property that it only appears once a document is deep enough to hit it, which may be in production.
    -
      name: Adopt JSON Schema, or a subset of it, instead of a SYON schema
      reason: It would bring an ecosystem, and it brings a validation language far larger than a type system, with constraint keywords ADR sheni_04 deliberately refused. A schema written in SYON is also the project dogfooding its own format, as ADR syon_01 did for the ADRs themselves.
